{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 18, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the origins of facebook and the complex web of ambition, betrayal, and innovation that surrounded its creation. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie is a gripping blend of courtroom drama and character study, anchored by a brilliant performance from jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg. eisenberg captures zuckerberg’s intensity, social awkwardness, and relentless drive with uncanny precision, making him both sympathetic and infuriating — a perfect reflection of the contradictions at the heart of the story.\n\nthe pacing is relentless, driven by sorkin’s razor-sharp dialogue that crackles with wit and intellectual tension. every line feels purposeful, layered with subtext, and delivered with impeccable timing. the supporting cast is equally strong: andrew garfield brings vulnerability and heart as eduardo saverin, zuckerberg’s best friend and early partner, while jesse plemons and armie hammer deliver chillingly calm portrayals of the winklevoss twins, whose legal battle against facebook becomes a central narrative thread.\n\ndanny elfman’s score, paired with the iconic opening track “handshake” by the range, sets a tone of restless energy and digital isolation that permeates the entire film. fincher’s visual style — cool, precise, and shadow-drenched — mirrors the sterile, high-stakes world of tech startups and ivy league privilege. the use of split screens and rapid-fire montages during coding sequences transforms programming into something cinematic and thrilling, elevating the mundane into the epic.\n\nbeyond its technical brilliance, the film is a profound meditation on connection in the digital age. it asks whether true friendship can survive ambition, whether innovation justifies betrayal, and whether the pursuit of connection through technology ultimately leads to loneliness. the courtroom scenes, framed as flashbacks, serve not just as narrative devices but as mirrors to the moral ambiguity of zuckerberg’s journey.\n\nthe social network isn’t a biopic in the traditional sense — it’s a modern tragedy dressed in hoodies and code. it doesn’t glorify zuckerberg, nor does it vilify him outright. instead, it presents him as a product of his time, a genius haunted by rejection and driven by a need to be seen. by the end, you’re left with a haunting sense of loss — not just for the friendships that were shattered, but for the idealism that was sacrificed in the name of scale.\n\nit’s a film that feels more relevant with each passing year, a cautionary tale wrapped in the sleek packaging of a tech origin story. the social network doesn’t just tell you how facebook was built — it shows you how the world changed because of it.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.9073468138230965e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0005541696446016431}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.3267362415790558}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.4560484290122986}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0008918836829252541}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.004169936757534742}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00012981049076188356}, {"token": " del", "logprob": -1.1497504711151123}, {"token": "ves", "logprob": -3.099436753473128e-06}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.007620786316692829}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -0.7098726034164429}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0001209901092806831}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.0002885640424210578}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.39542996883392334}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -7.867782187531702e-06}, {"token": " complex", "logprob": -0.639150857925415}, {"token":